<td style="text-align: center;">0</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td style="text-align: center;">1</td>
<td style="text-align: center;">0</td>
<td style="text-align: center;">0</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td style="text-align: center;">1</td>
<td style="text-align: center;">1</td>
<td style="text-align: center;">1</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Matrix form : <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%7B%5Ctext%7BXnor%7D%7D%20=%20%5Cbegin%7Bbmatrix%7D%0A0%20&amp;%201%20%5C%5C%0A1%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%0A1%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%0A0%20&amp;%201%0A%5Cend%7Bbmatrix%7D%0A"></p>
<hr>
</section>
</section>
<section id="matrix-representation" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="matrix-representation">Matrix representation</h3>
<p>You might be wondering what all these matrices actually contain. You can think of a matrix as a “box” that embodies a function.</p>
<p>They all share the exact same format:<br>
- <strong>The rows</strong> correspond to an enumeration of all possible input combinations.<br>
- <strong>The columns</strong> correspond to an enumeration of all possible output combinations.</p>
<p>If a function has <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?M"> binary inputs and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N"> binary outputs, the resulting matrix will have <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?2%5EM"> rows and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?2%5EN"> columns.</p>
<p>The column containing the value <strong>1</strong> marks the precise spot where a given input maps to its corresponding output.</p>
<p>For example, here is the matrix representation of the AND gate with the rows and column labels added:</p>
<div id="a42fd412" class="cell" data-execution_count="9">
<div class="cell-output cell-output-display cell-output-markdown" data-execution_count="449">
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7Br%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%5Ctext%7B%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%600''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%601''%7D%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6000''%7D%20&amp;%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6001''%7D%20&amp;%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6010''%7D%20&amp;%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6011''%7D%20&amp;%200%20&amp;%201%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20"></p>
</div>
</div>
<p>As expected, the output is <code>1</code>, only if both inputs are <code>1</code>.</p>
<p>Even the logical states follow the same format. You can think of a logical state as a function with zero input and one output (the logical state).</p>
<p>Logical <code>0</code>:</p>
<div id="ce376aac" class="cell" data-execution_count="10">
<div class="cell-output cell-output-display cell-output-markdown" data-execution_count="450">
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%5Ctext%7B%60%600''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%601''%7D%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20"></p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Logical <code>1</code>:</p>
<div id="56130d67" class="cell" data-execution_count="11">
<div class="cell-output cell-output-display cell-output-markdown" data-execution_count="451">
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%5Ctext%7B%60%600''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%601''%7D%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%200%20&amp;%201%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20"></p>
</div>
</div>
<hr>
</section>
<section id="matrix-arithmetic" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="matrix-arithmetic">Matrix arithmetic</h3>
<p>The beauty of this representation is that functions can be chained together end-to-end using standard matrix multiplication.</p>
<p>For example, if we have a state corresponding to the bit “1” and pass it through a NOT gate, we can simply compute: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctext%7BOne%7D%20%5Ccdot%20%5Ctext%7BNOT%7D">.</p>
<div id="650268c3" class="cell" data-execution_count="12">
<div class="cell-output cell-output-display cell-output-markdown">
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%20%5Chline%200%20&amp;%201%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20%20%5Ccdot%20%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%20%5Chline%200%20&amp;%201%20%5C%5C%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20%20=%20%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%20%5Chline%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20%20"></p>
</div>
</div>
<p>And this yields the expected result: Zero.</p>
<p>For logic gates with two inputs, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?B">, one might naively expect something like <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%20%5Ccdot%20B%20%5Ccdot%20%5Ctext%7BAND%7D">. However, that will not work dimensionally. <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?B"> are <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?1%20%5Ctimes%202"> matrices, whereas the <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctext%7BAND%7D"> gate is a <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?4%20%5Ctimes%202"> matrix.</p>
<p>To give the inputs <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(A,%20B)"> the proper dimension to interface with the AND gate, they must first be combined using the <strong>Kronecker tensor product</strong> (<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%20%5Cotimes%20B">).</p>
<div id="1caf8257" class="cell" data-execution_count="13">
<div class="cell-output cell-output-display cell-output-markdown">
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%5Ctext%7B%60%600''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%601''%7D%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20%20%5Cotimes%20%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%5Ctext%7B%60%600''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%601''%7D%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%200%20&amp;%201%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20%20=%20%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccccc%7C%7D%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6000''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6001''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6010''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6011''%7D%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%200%20&amp;%201%20&amp;%200%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20%20"></p>
</div>
</div>
<p>Once the inputs are combined into a single matrix, we can multiply them directly by the logic gate:</p>
<div id="9a767294" class="cell" data-execution_count="14">
<div class="cell-output cell-output-display cell-output-markdown">
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccccc%7C%7D%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6000''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6001''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6010''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6011''%7D%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%200%20&amp;%200%20&amp;%200%20&amp;%201%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20%20%5Ccdot%20%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7Br%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%5Ctext%7B%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%600''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%601''%7D%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6000''%7D%20&amp;%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6001''%7D%20&amp;%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6010''%7D%20&amp;%201%20&amp;%200%20%5C%5C%20%5Ctext%7B%60%6011''%7D%20&amp;%200%20&amp;%201%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20%20=%20%20%5Cbegin%7Barray%7D%7B%7Ccc%7C%7D%20%5Ctext%7B%60%600''%7D%20&amp;%20%5Ctext%7B%60%601''%7D%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%200%20&amp;%201%20%5C%5C%20%20%5Chline%20%5Cend%7Barray%7D%20%20"></p>
</div>
</div>
<hr>


</section>

 ]]></description>
